  "blockers": [
    {
      "id": "BLK-001",
      "text": "Firestore writes to calls/detections are rejected with permission-denied against the real trace-180 project",
      "added": "2026-09-03T21:17:50Z",
      "status": "OPEN",
      "owner": "Live smoke test (node script using the real NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_* config) confirmed the Firebase app connects successfully to trace-180 and a listener receives the local optimistic write, but the server rejects the write (Code 7 PERMISSION_DENIED) because no firestore.rules file exists anywhere in this repo. This is not a defect in SHIELD's code -- firestore.ts correctly builds/validates/sends the write and correctly surfaces the resulting error as the 'not syncing' banner. Per ARCHITECTURE.md this security-rules file is explicitly a shared, additive-only artifact across SHIELD/TRACK/AGENT, so SHIELD did not unilaterally create one. Someone on the team needs to deploy Firestore rules that allow writes to calls/detections (and reads of users) before S5's cross-tab acceptance test can be run for real."
    },
    {
      "id": "BLK-002",
      "text": "Firestore rules on trace-180 allow create on calls/detections but deny update/delete for the anon web client (confirmed via live write test). writeCall uses setDoc merge, an update after the first write, so risk updates will fail to sync past the first write until rules change. CLI account dronicsacademy@gmail.com lacks IAM permission on trace-180 to inspect/fix rules from here.",
      "added": "2026-09-03T21:48:20Z",
      "status": "OPEN",
      "owner": "orchestrator"
    }
  ],
